 2. Initialize the state generator with the reconstruction solver dictionary, unknown scatterers, surfaces, numerical parameters, sources, Stokes counts, and background scatterers: `state_gen = at3d.medium.StateGenerator(solvers_reconstruct, unknown_scatterers, surfaces, numerical_parameters, sources, num_stokes, background_optical_scatterers)` [S2, unofficial].
 **Parameters:**
-- `cloud_mask`: boolean array identifying cloud regions, derived from `solvers[wvl].medium['cloud'].extinction.data > 0` [S2, unofficial].
 - `uncertainty_model`: radiometric noise uncertainty applied to sensors, initialized with parameters 1e-5 and 1e-3 [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [Kerbl et al., 2023] provides a robust baseline for static scene reconstruction. However, dynamic scenes, specific geometric constraints, and occlusion handling require specialized adaptations. [GauHuman](9198-gauhuman.md) and [GaussianBody](9209-gaussianbody.md) address the long-standing problem of dynamic human avatars from monocular video, where depth ambiguity leads to artifacts in clothing and body shape. [Inpainting for Displaced Objects](9523-inpainting-for-displaced-objects.md) addresses the specific failure mode of 3DGS when objects move, exposing unseen background. [SplattingAvatar](9556-splattingavatar.md) integrates 3DGS with parametric meshes (FLAME) to provide stable, controllable avatars. These methods represent a shift from generic scene representation to domain-aware reconstruction, leveraging the differentiable nature of 3DGS to incorporate physical, geometric, or generative priors.
